      "content": "Object-oriented Programming (OOP)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In this chapter, we will learn about object-oriented programming (OOP) and how to implement it in our code."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Object-oriented programming (OOP) is a popular technique to solve programming problems by creating objects."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Let's try to understand it with an example."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Suppose we need to store the name and the test score of University students. And, based on the test score, we need to find if a student passed or failed the examination. Then, the structure of our code would look something like this."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "How to use OOP?",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now imagine we have to store the name and the test score of multiple students instead of one student."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "If we were to use the same approach, we can use the same check_pass_fail() function, however, we need to create variables to store the name and the score for each student. This would make our code less organized and messy."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "How to use OOP?",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Since these data and functions are related, it would be better if we could treat them as a single entity. We can do exactly that by creating objects. This approach to create objects to solve problems is known as object-oriented programming."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Next, we will see how we use objects to solve this problem."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
